(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-gi-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"GI Physiology — Quiz 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Slow waves in gastrointestinal smooth muscle determine the...","choices":["the rate of gastric acid secretion coming from the stomach's parietal cells during a meal","maximum frequency and timing of contractions, though not every wave triggers one","rate at which the exocrine pancreas releases its digestive enzymes into the gut","absolute strength of every contraction regardless of neural input"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow waves are rhythmic oscillations in membrane potential set by the interstitial cells of Cajal; they set when contractions can occur, but an actual contraction happens only if the wave reaches threshold with spike potentials, so they pace but do not force every contraction. — GI Physiology, slides 35-39"},{"q":"The enteric nervous system is notable because it...","choices":["can coordinate motility and secretion largely on its own, independent of the CNS","operates solely through circulating hormones in the blood rather than through any neurons","controls only the secretion of gastric acid and nothing else along the gut wall","requires constant input from the brain to function at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The enteric nervous system, with its myenteric and submucosal plexuses, contains enough neurons to integrate reflexes and control motility and secretion by itself; the autonomic nervous system modulates it, but the gut can function even without central input. — GI Physiology, slides 24-33"},{"q":"As a meal enters the stomach, receptive relaxation allows it to...","choices":["secrete bile to begin the early emulsification of dietary fat","immediately propel all contents into the duodenum at once","accommodate a large volume with little rise in internal pressure","absorb most nutrients before they reach the small intestine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A vagovagal reflex relaxes the orad stomach (mediated by NO and VIP) so it can expand to hold a meal without a large pressure increase, storing food for gradual processing and controlled emptying. — GI Physiology, slides 44, 46"},{"q":"The peristaltic reflex ('law of the gut') moves contents aborally by...","choices":["contracting the entire intestine at once to squeeze contents forward","relying on sympathetic input to drive rhythmic forward propulsion","contracting the segment behind the bolus while relaxing the segment ahead","alternating haustral pouches that slowly knead contents toward the anus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: distension causes contraction orad (behind) with receptive relaxation downstream, propelling contents forward. — GI Physiology, slide 39"},{"q":"When acidic, fatty chyme enters the duodenum, hormonal signals act to...","choices":["stop all pancreatic secretion until the stomach empties in most cases","accelerate gastric emptying to clear the stomach quickly","trigger immediate defecation of the intestinal contents","slow gastric emptying so the small intestine is not overwhelmed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fat and acid in the duodenum trigger enterogastric signals (including CCK and secretin) that slow gastric emptying; this ensures the duodenum has time to neutralize acid and digest fat before receiving more chyme. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 46"},{"q":"Cholecystokinin, released in response to fat and protein in the duodenum, acts to...","choices":["stimulate parietal cells to secrete their maximal load of acid","contract the gallbladder and stimulate pancreatic enzyme secretion","trigger mass movements that empty the distal colon in the body under most conditions","relax the gallbladder while inhibiting all pancreatic secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CCK from duodenal I-cells is the principal signal that contracts the gallbladder (and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi) while strongly stimulating pancreatic enzyme release, delivering bile and enzymes to digest fat and protein. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15"},{"q":"When acidic chyme enters the duodenum, the hormone secretin is released and acts to...","choices":["speed gastric emptying so more chyme enters quickly","stimulate the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate that neutralizes the acid","increase gastric acid secretion to aid digestion","contract the gallbladder to release bile as it is usually described here"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secretin, released from duodenal cells in response to acid, signals the pancreas (and liver) to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid; this neutralizes the acidic chyme, protecting the intestinal lining and creating the alkaline pH that pancreatic enzymes require. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 100-105"},{"q":"Late in a meal, as gastric contents become very acidic, acid secretion declines because...","choices":["low pH triggers somatostatin, which suppresses gastrin release","secretin from the stomach directly stimulates further gastrin output","histamine is depleted faster than ECL cells can regenerate it","parietal cells run out of the carbonic anhydrase needed to make acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: falling pH stimulates somatostatin and directly inhibits gastrin, providing negative feedback that reduces acid output. — GI Physiology, slides 90, 94-95"},{"q":"The gastric phase of secretion, which occurs once food reaches the stomach, is triggered mainly by...","choices":["the sight and smell of food before it is eaten","acidic chyme arriving in the duodenum as it is usually described here","distension of the stomach wall and the presence of protein products","a fall in blood glucose between meals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When food enters the stomach, distension of the wall and peptides from partially digested protein stimulate local reflexes, vagovagal reflexes, and gastrin release, driving a large portion of the acid and enzyme secretion during the meal. — GI Physiology, slides 92-96"},{"q":"The pancreatic bicarbonate response to a meal is driven mainly by...","choices":["gastrin, released as protein stretches the stomach wall","insulin, released as blood glucose rises after eating","motilin, released cyclically during the fasting state","secretin, released when acidic chyme lowers the duodenal pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When acidic chyme enters the duodenum and pH falls below about 4.5, S-cells release secretin, which stimulates the pancreatic ducts to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes the acid and protects the mucosa. — GI Physiology, slides 106-109"},{"q":"The pancreas avoids digesting itself largely because...","choices":["enzymes are secreted so slowly that ongoing tissue repair always manages to keep pace","pancreatic tissue simply lacks any receptors for its own digestive enzymes entirely","proteases are stored and secreted as inactive zymogens activated only in the gut","bicarbonate keeps the acinar pH too high for any enzyme to function"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: enzymes like trypsinogen are stored inactive (with a trypsin inhibitor) and activated only in the intestine. — GI Physiology, slides 103-105"},{"q":"Pancreatic proteases such as trypsin are secreted as inactive precursors that are then activated...","choices":["in the intestinal lumen, so the pancreas is not digested by its own enzymes","only after the food has entered the large intestine as it typically occurs here","by stomach acid before they reach the small intestine","within the pancreatic cells immediately after synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Proteases are released as zymogens (e.g., trypsinogen) and activated only after reaching the small intestine — enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the others. This delay protects the pancreas from self-digestion. — GI Physiology, slides 101-105"},{"q":"Saliva aids the earliest stage of digestion by...","choices":["lubricating food and beginning starch breakdown with salivary amylase","neutralizing stomach acid before food is swallowed","emulsifying dietary fat into small droplets during this process in the tissues","completing the digestion of protein into amino acids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Saliva moistens and lubricates food to ease swallowing, dissolves substances for taste, begins carbohydrate digestion with salivary amylase, and contains lysozyme with mild antibacterial action — launching digestion in the mouth. — GI Physiology, slides 76-81"},{"q":"Within the gastric glands, the division of labor is such that...","choices":["both cell types secrete only mucus to protect the lining during this particular process","parietal cells secrete pepsinogen while chief cells secrete acid","chief cells secrete bile and parietal cells secrete enzymes","chief cells secrete pepsinogen while parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chief cells release pepsinogen (activated to pepsin by acid), while parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid (which also activates pepsinogen and kills microbes) and intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 absorption. Each cell type has a distinct secretory role. — GI Physiology, slides 82-90"},{"q":"Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the intestinal brush border initiates protein digestion by...","choices":["emulsifying protein aggregates so that pepsin can reach them","converting trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the other proteases","activating pancreatic amylase so that it can begin breaking down starch","cleaving dietary protein directly into individual absorbable free amino acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which autocatalytically activates chymotrypsinogen and procarboxypeptidase. — GI Physiology, slide 104"},{"q":"Pepsin works best in the stomach because it...","choices":["digests dietary carbohydrates rather than protein in the stomach","requires the alkaline environment created by bile","functions only after being neutralized in the duodenum","is a protease with an acidic optimal pH provided by gastric HCl"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pepsin is a protein-digesting enzyme with an acidic pH optimum (roughly 1.8–3.5); gastric HCl both activates pepsinogen to pepsin and provides the low pH environment in which pepsin is most active, so it functions well in the stomach and is inactivated in the alkaline duodenum. — GI Physiology, slides 82-85"},{"q":"Bile salts emulsify fat effectively because each molecule...","choices":["is entirely water-soluble and dissolves the fat outright","has both a water-soluble and a fat-soluble end (it is amphipathic)","binds oxygen to help oxidize the fat for energy in the body's tissues","chemically digests the triglyceride into fatty acids itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bile salts are amphipathic: one part associates with water and the other with lipid. This lets them coat fat droplets and break them into many tiny droplets, greatly increasing the surface area available for lipase to act on. — GI Physiology, slides 130-133"},{"q":"Bile salts aid fat absorption because their amphipathic structure lets them...","choices":["emulsify fat and ferry lipid digestion products to the mucosa as micelles","actively pump fatty acids across the enterocyte membrane using ATP","convert dietary cholesterol into water-soluble bile pigments for excretion","chemically hydrolyze triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: being amphipathic, bile salts emulsify fat and form micelles that carry monoglycerides and fatty acids to the mucosa. — GI Physiology, slide 20"},{"q":"Enterohepatic circulation conserves bile salts by...","choices":["converting them into cholesterol for storage in the gallbladder","excreting nearly all of them in the feces after a single use","secreting them into the urine for later reabsorption by the kidney","reabsorbing most of them in the ileum and returning them to the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 95% of secreted bile salts are actively reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, enter the portal blood, and are taken back up by hepatocytes for re-secretion, so the same pool is reused several times per meal and only a small fraction is lost in feces. — GI Physiology (bile), Ch 71 Liver, slide 25"},{"q":"After fatty acids are reassembled into triglycerides inside the enterocyte, they...","choices":["bind intrinsic factor and are carried to the ileum for their later active uptake","are packaged into chylomicrons that enter lymph rather than blood capillaries","diffuse directly into portal blood for immediate transport to the liver","are stored in the brush border until bile salts release them to plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: triglycerides are packaged into chylomicrons, which are too large for capillaries and enter lacteals/lymph, reaching blood via the thoracic duct. — GI Physiology, slides 135-137"},{"q":"The gastrocolic reflex helps explain why...","choices":["eating a meal often stimulates the urge to defecate","the stomach empties immediately when the colon fills","bile is released the moment food enters the mouth","nutrients are absorbed faster in the large intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The gastrocolic reflex links stomach filling to increased colonic motility: distension of the stomach triggers mass movements in the colon that propel contents toward the rectum, which is why a meal frequently prompts the urge to defecate. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"Bacteria residing in the colon contribute usefully to the host by...","choices":["digesting and absorbing dietary fat for the body under most normal conditions","producing hydrochloric acid to sterilize the stool","synthesizing certain vitamins, including vitamin K, that can be absorbed","secreting insulin to regulate the body's glucose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The large intestine's resident bacteria ferment undigested material and synthesize several vitamins — notably vitamin K and some B vitamins — a portion of which the colon absorbs, supplementing the dietary supply. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"The colon's pouch-like haustra are formed and their contractions serve to...","choices":["secrete digestive enzymes to finish breaking down food","absorb the bulk of dietary fat and protein","mix and slowly move contents while water is absorbed","rapidly propel contents straight to the rectum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The haustra are sacculations produced by the colon's muscle arrangement; their slow, segmenting contractions (haustration) knead the contents and expose them to the mucosa for water and electrolyte absorption, gradually moving material along toward the rectum. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"The liver prepares bilirubin for excretion by...","choices":["binding it tightly to intrinsic factor for its absorption in the ileum","conjugating it with glucuronic acid to make it water-soluble for bile","converting it into a bile salt that emulsifies dietary fat","oxidizing it into iron that is then returned to the bone marrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hepatocytes conjugate lipid-soluble (unconjugated) bilirubin with glucuronic acid, converting it to a water-soluble (conjugated) form that can be excreted into bile; impaired conjugation or excretion causes jaundice. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-16"},{"q":"During fasting, the liver helps maintain blood glucose by gluconeogenesis, which is the...","choices":["absorption of glucose directly from the digested food","breakdown of stored glycogen into glucose molecules during this process in the tissues","conversion of glucose into fat for long-term storage","synthesis of new glucose from noncarbohydrate sources such as amino acids and lactate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gluconeogenesis is the liver's production of glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors — amino acids (from protein), lactate, and glycerol — sustaining blood glucose once glycogen stores are exhausted during fasting, which is vital for glucose-dependent tissues like the brain. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9; Ch 68, slides 25-27"},{"q":"The small intestine's absorptive surface is vastly increased by...","choices":["thick muscular walls that grind the food finely under most normal conditions","a single large chamber that holds food for a long time","circular folds, villi, and microvilli that multiply the surface area","a smooth, flat lining that speeds the passage of food"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three levels of folding — the circular folds of the wall, the finger-like villi, and the microvilli of the brush border — together increase the absorptive surface area enormously, maximizing contact between nutrients and the absorptive cells. — GI Physiology, slides 120-122, 140"},{"q":"Adequate absorption of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K depends on...","choices":["an acidic intestinal pH created by gastric secretions","the presence of bile salts to incorporate them into micelles","a specialized active pump unique to each vitamin in this scenario","conversion into water-soluble forms before uptake"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fat-soluble vitamins are absorbed along with dietary fat; bile salts are needed to form the micelles that ferry them to the enterocyte surface. Impaired bile flow therefore reduces uptake of these vitamins. — GI Physiology, slides 132-133, 144"},{"q":"Micelles improve fat absorption by...","choices":["ferrying poorly soluble fatty acids and monoglycerides to the enterocyte surface","actively transporting whole fat droplets across the cell membrane","chemically converting the triglycerides into small water-soluble sugars for uptake","carrying absorbed fat directly into the portal blood as chylomicrons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bile-salt micelles solubilize the products of fat digestion (fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol) and shuttle them across the unstirred water layer to the brush border, where they are released to diffuse into the enterocyte. — GI Physiology, slides 132-133"},{"q":"Glucose absorption against its gradient is powered indirectly by...","choices":["the sodium gradient maintained by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase","the concentration gradient of fructose in the lumen","bile-salt micelles physically pulling the glucose into the cell","direct hydrolysis of ATP by the glucose carrier itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glucose enters the enterocyte on the SGLT1 cotransporter, riding the inward sodium gradient; that gradient is set up by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, so glucose uptake is secondary active transport ultimately powered by ATP used to pump sodium. — GI Physiology, slide 126"},{"q":"Water is absorbed across the intestinal wall mainly by...","choices":["active transport pumps that move water directly against gradients","binding to bile salts that carry it into the enterocyte","digestion of water molecules by brush-border enzymes during this process","osmosis, following the absorption of solutes such as sodium and glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Water absorption is passive: as sodium, glucose, amino acids, and other solutes are absorbed, they raise the osmotic pull on the blood side, and water follows by osmosis. This is why efficient solute uptake drives water uptake. — GI Physiology, slides 140-143"}]};
